fix: Print the session hint only when the refusal would not say why

Three review findings on the ErrBadRequest branch, all on the same few lines.

The hint fired on a refusal that HAD explained itself. The condition was "a
session was asked for and the words do not mention sessions", so
`--session s1 --since bogus` printed "the proxy did not name the sessions it
holds" underneath the proxy's own perfectly clear complaint about since,
sending the reader to look at sessions when since was the problem.

What the branch wants to know is whether any detail arrived at all, and getBody
already knows: it either wraps the server's words or does not. Carry that as an
exported ErrNoDetail sentinel beside ErrBadRequest and check it with errors.Is,
rather than matching on either the server's wording or getBody's own formatting.
It is wrapped in a type that adds nothing to the message, because the text is
printed to a user and "bad request: apiclient: no detail" reads worse than the
bare refusal it would replace. errors.Is(err, ErrBadRequest) is unaffected.

The hint's curl was not runnable. It printed the raw --endpoint, so
`--endpoint http://host:47601/` produced `host:47601//v1/sessions`, which
ServeMux answers with a 307 whose HTML body is all `curl -s` shows -- the one
fallback meant to rescue a dead end leading into another. Keep the client and
print Endpoint(), the trimmed form the request itself uses.

And the Export doc comment claimed a failed walk "simply ends the body, which
NDJSON tolerates". It resets the connection as of #1830, so the caller sees an
error rather than a short success; the help text now says the command exits
non-zero and what the output holds.

Tests: a refusal that named a different problem gets no hint, a trailing-slash
endpoint produces no doubled slash, a truncated export exits 1, and ErrNoDetail
is pinned across all three 400 shapes -- parseable, undocumented, and cut by the
512-byte read. The first two go red against the old conditions.

Reported by clawgenti and huang195.

